          "description": "Reference run: WRF 4.8 real-terrain simulation of data/eqwefic/notes/gwdo_real_reference_run.md (Last Chance Gulch d01, Colorado Rockies, 8.1 km, 150 x 180 x 50, dt = 45 s, gwd_opt = 1 with ELVMAX supplied by WPS util/compute_gwdo.py -- without it GWDO is silently off everywhere, FORTRAN_BUGS B17). Step 2 = 06-25 12:01Z, global row j = 81, tile column 30 of the dump that starts at i = 76. THE column FORTRAN_BUGS N85 was measured on. Its one saturating level sits at Ri = 0.25664, 1.6e-3 above the critical value ric = 0.25, so the saturation factor 2 sqrt(temc) - temc is only 1.301e-2 and a perturbation of Ri is amplified 37-fold in the stress. Weak orography: ELVMAX = 932 m, var = 13.9 m, Fr = 0.064, launch stress 0.012 Pa. Reference build: the real64 (kind_phys = real64) replay of the same dumped row through kernels/gwdo_diag_driver of WRF fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-gwdo, with the six scalar constants g, cp, R_d, R_v, eps_1 and pi supplied at binary64 precision instead of WRF's real32 module values (gwdo_real/mkflat64.py). They are subroutine arguments, not internal parameters, so that is what a double-precision kernel reference means for this scheme, and it is exactly what lib/wrf_constants.esm carries; promoting them flips no branch on any of the 140 dumped rows and moves the outputs by a median 2.7e-8 of the row maximum. Every assertion is on an algebraic observed at t = 0 and the document declares no ODE state, so the empty time_span is a single static evaluation.",
