          "id": "s720_j101_i39_col3_daytime_mixed_layer_drag_off",
          "description": "Reference run: WRF 4.8 real-terrain simulation of data/eqwefic/notes/gwdo_real_reference_run.md (Last Chance Gulch d01, Colorado Rockies, 8.1 km, 150 x 180 x 50, dt = 45 s, gwd_opt = 1 with ELVMAX supplied by WPS util/compute_gwdo.py -- without it GWDO is silently off everywhere, FORTRAN_BUGS B17). Step 720 = 06-25 21Z (15:00 local), global row j = 101, tile column 3 of the dump that starts at i = 39. The all-off branch: ELVMAX = 3304 m and var = 361 m clear the two orography gates by a wide margin, but the afternoon mixed layer is statically unstable below the reference level, so the low-level mean N^2 is -1.71e-5 s^-2 and the sixth condition of ldrag fires. Every dumped column of the 17Z, 19Z and 21Z rows is off this way. NOTE that the FIRST condition, omax <= 50 m, is not exercised by any test: with ELVMAX supplied it is 231-4227 m across the domain. That condition is precisely FORTRAN_BUGS B17 -- under WPS 4.3, which writes no ELVMAX, it is the only one that ever fires and it fires everywhere. Reference build: the real64 (kind_phys = real64) replay of the same dumped row through kernels/gwdo_diag_driver of WRF fork branch earthsciml-instrumented-gwdo, with the six scalar constants g, cp, R_d, R_v, eps_1 and pi supplied at binary64 precision instead of WRF's real32 module values (gwdo_real/mkflat64.py). They are subroutine arguments, not internal parameters, so that is what a double-precision kernel reference means for this scheme, and it is exactly what lib/wrf_constants.esm carries; promoting them flips no branch on any of the 140 dumped rows and moves the outputs by a median 2.7e-8 of the row maximum. Every assertion is on an algebraic observed at t = 0 and the document declares no ODE state, so the empty time_span is a single static evaluation.",
